<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
  Copyright (c) 2026 Emerson Lopes and PowerRustCOBOL contributors

  The Windows bundle (WiX 3 Burn), spec 112 task T19, decision D12.

  WHY IT EXISTS. The setup prepares the platform only when a linker is installed, and the linker is Microsoft's C++
  build tools. They cannot be installed from inside product.wxs's package: Windows Installer runs one installation
  at a time and Microsoft's bootstrapper answers 1618 inside it (plan section 9, measured). So the file a
  developer downloads, PowerRustCOBOL-<version>-setup.exe, is this bundle: it runs a small program first,
  prc-buildtools.exe, which installs the build tools when this computer has none, and then installs the same .msi
  that is also published on its own. The .msi stays a complete installer: a silent or managed install uses it
  directly, and it says plainly when the linker is missing.

  WHAT THE FIRST PACKAGE DOES. prc-buildtools.exe (crates/cobolt-setup/src/buildtools.rs and bin/prc_buildtools.rs)
  decides for itself, so the bundle chains it every time: nothing to do when the tools are there; otherwise it
  downloads Microsoft's installer, checks that Microsoft signed it, runs it and waits. Its exit code is the whole
  protocol, and the ExitCode rows below are held to the program's constants by a test (installer.rs):
    0     go on with the .msi
    3010  go on, and ask Windows for a restart at the end
    1602  the developer cancelled: end the bundle
    1618  another installation is running, which the .msi could not run beside either: end the bundle
  Every other outcome of the build tools is 0: a Build Tools install that did not happen is not a reason to withhold
  the program, and the .msi's finish page names the missing linker and what to do.

  WHAT THE DEVELOPER SEES. WixStdBA's own page (a licence-link page, nothing else to choose): what the setup will
  do, a link to Microsoft's licence terms, and a box that has to be ticked, which is the acceptance of those terms
  for the build tools (spec 112 Q4). Then the .msi's own wizard (DisplayInternalUI), the same pages as installing
  the .msi alone.

  Built with:
    candle -arch x64 -ext WixBalExtension -dVer=<x.y.z> -dMsiFile=<the .msi> -dBuildToolsExe=<prc-buildtools.exe>
           bundle.wxs
    light  -ext WixBalExtension -cultures:en-us -loc bundle.wxl bundle.wixobj -o <setup.exe>

  Every string in this file is ASCII. Prose belongs in comments.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/wix/2006/wi"
     xmlns:bal="http://schemas.microsoft.com/wix/BalExtension">
  <!-- UpgradeCode is FIXED for the bundle's whole life, like the .msi's; the two differ (a bundle is not a
       product). -->
  <Bundle Name="PowerRustCOBOL AI" Version="$(var.Ver)" Manufacturer="Emerson Lopes"
          UpgradeCode="B2D4F6A8-1C3E-4A5B-9D7F-0E2C4A6B8D10">
    <BootstrapperApplicationRef Id="WixStandardBootstrapperApplication.HyperlinkLicense">
      <bal:WixStandardBootstrapperApplication
          LicenseUrl="https://visualstudio.microsoft.com/license-terms/"
          SuppressOptionsUI="yes"
          SuppressRepair="yes" />
    </BootstrapperApplicationRef>

    <!-- The one choice the .msi's page does not make for a silent install: pass it through, as
         setup.exe /quiet PREPARE_PLATFORM=1. Empty means "not asked", which the .msi reads as unset. -->
    <Variable Name="PREPARE_PLATFORM" Type="string" Value="" bal:Overridable="yes" />

    <Chain>
      <!-- Always chained, and permanent: the program decides whether there is anything to do, and it is never
           "uninstalled" (the build tools are the developer's, whoever installed them). -->
      <ExePackage Id="BuildTools" Name="prc-buildtools.exe" SourceFile="$(var.BuildToolsExe)"
                  Compressed="yes" PerMachine="yes" Permanent="yes" Vital="yes">
        <ExitCode Value="0" Behavior="success" />
        <ExitCode Value="3010" Behavior="scheduleReboot" />
        <ExitCode Value="1602" Behavior="error" />
        <ExitCode Value="1618" Behavior="error" />
      </ExePackage>

      <!-- The same .msi that is published alone. Its own wizard is shown (DisplayInternalUI); the bundle's
           entry in Apps and features replaces the .msi's own (ARPSYSTEMCOMPONENT), so there is one
           PowerRustCOBOL AI to uninstall, not two. -->
      <MsiPackage Id="PowerRustCOBOL" SourceFile="$(var.MsiFile)" Compressed="yes" Vital="yes"
                  DisplayInternalUI="yes" ForcePerMachine="yes">
        <MsiProperty Name="PREPARE_PLATFORM" Value="[PREPARE_PLATFORM]" />
        <MsiProperty Name="ARPSYSTEMCOMPONENT" Value="1" />
      </MsiPackage>
    </Chain>
  </Bundle>
</Wix>
